WEBVTT

00:00.800 --> 00:04.620
This video shows how to install DataLab on a Windows PC.

00:04.620 --> 00:06.700
First, what to have ready.

00:06.700 --> 00:11.900
Then, how to download DataLab and run its setup, and where your key and password go.

00:11.900 --> 00:15.608
And last, how to open DataLab, and check it's working.

00:16.808 --> 00:19.208
Everything starts at the DataLab website.

00:19.208 --> 00:20.408
Have these ready.

00:20.408 --> 00:25.408
On Windows, first activate privileged access on your Michigan Medicine profile page:

00:25.408 --> 00:28.108
setup needs administrator permission once.

00:28.108 --> 00:34.488
You'll also need your University of Michigan GPT Toolkit API key and the database password,

00:34.488 --> 00:38.548
both in the email from Aman; the University of Michigan VPN;

00:38.548 --> 00:42.152
and your GitHub account, for the lab's shared repositories.

00:43.352 --> 00:46.832
Under Install on Windows, press Download for Windows.

00:47.332 --> 00:51.452
In your Downloads folder, right-click the ZIP and choose Extract All.

00:51.452 --> 00:54.892
Choose Downloads as the destination, and press Extract.

00:54.892 --> 00:56.716
Then go back to the website.

00:57.216 --> 00:59.836
Copy the setup command with the Copy button.

00:59.836 --> 01:04.944
Then open PowerShell: press Start, type PowerShell, and press Enter.

01:05.444 --> 01:07.624
Paste the command and press Enter.

01:07.624 --> 01:09.644
Setup works through eight steps.

01:09.644 --> 01:13.324
Step one gets Windows ready: DataLab runs in Docker,

01:13.324 --> 01:18.380
a sealed-off space on your computer, and Docker needs a Windows feature called WSL.

01:18.880 --> 01:23.800
The first time, setup lists what Windows needs and asks for administrator permission.

01:23.800 --> 01:26.960
Press Enter, then click Yes when Windows asks.

01:26.960 --> 01:32.152
Windows then needs a restart, and setup carries on by itself after you sign in.

01:32.652 --> 01:34.852
Step two starts Docker Desktop.

01:34.852 --> 01:37.492
If it shows a welcome screen, you can skip it.

01:37.492 --> 01:41.072
Docker only needs to be running: it isn't DataLab itself,

01:41.072 --> 01:43.792
so you won't find anything about the study inside it.

01:43.792 --> 01:47.796
Steps three to five install DataLab and download what it needs.

01:48.996 --> 01:52.636
Step six asks for your key, then your database password.

01:52.636 --> 01:55.576
Paste each one from Aman's email and press Enter.

01:55.576 --> 01:57.596
Each character shows as a star,

01:57.596 --> 02:01.376
and both are saved in Windows Credential Manager, not in any file.

02:01.376 --> 02:04.236
If you skip them now, add them later in DataLab,

02:04.236 --> 02:06.996
under Settings and Safety, then Connections.

02:07.496 --> 02:09.996
Step seven offers to sign you in to GitHub,

02:09.996 --> 02:14.756
for the lab's knowledge base and pipelines; you can also do that later, in Settings.

02:14.756 --> 02:18.396
Step eight adds DataLab to the Start menu and your Desktop.

02:18.396 --> 02:19.784
Setup is done.

02:20.984 --> 02:25.784
To open DataLab, press Start, type DataLab, and press Enter.

02:25.784 --> 02:28.760
There's also a DataLab shortcut on your Desktop.

02:29.260 --> 02:33.420
It opens a PowerShell window of its own: leave that open while you work.

02:33.420 --> 02:35.836
Then your browser opens on DataLab.

02:36.336 --> 02:37.636
This is DataLab.

02:37.636 --> 02:41.436
Connect to the VPN, and check the buttons at the top right.

02:41.436 --> 02:44.576
If the key or password is missing, or you skipped it,

02:44.576 --> 02:47.656
add it in Settings and Safety, under Connections.

02:47.656 --> 02:50.040
The video Getting connected shows how.

02:50.540 --> 02:52.300
That's DataLab installed.

02:52.300 --> 02:55.748
There's more on the website, under A little help getting started.
